Yeah, that became, yeah, come on, what you, what, you don't, it was something like, you don't need to say this to me, I'm not, you know, try to impress me.
是的，这变成了，是的，来吧，你什么，什么，你不，就像是，你不需要对我说这个，我不是，你知道，试图给我留下深刻的印象。

00:17:52.039 --> 00:17:55.400
And I remember you kept going like, no, I really didn't go there.
我记得你一直说，不，我真的没有去那里。

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:56.440
I did, I went there.
我做到了，我去了那里。

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:59.839
Yeah, I know, because you say back when I was at Stanford all the time.
是的，我知道，因为你总是说我在斯坦福大学的时候。

00:17:59.839 --> 00:18:06.440
But the idea that Chris had the guest pre-written on, like, sort of like a newly-wet game style card.
但克里斯让客人预先写在上面的想法，就像是一张新湿的游戏风格的卡片。

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.440
It's like Johnny Carson's Envelope.
就像约翰尼·卡森的信封一样。

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.440
It's the absolute great con act.
这绝对是伟大的骗局。

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:14.440
Like, with the fact that Chris just did that, Chris was just Marshall in this episode.
就像克里斯刚刚这么做的事实一样，克里斯在这一集中只是马歇尔。

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:15.440
Yeah.
是的。

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:16.440
Chris was just the game master.
克里斯只是游戏大师。

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:17.440
He held up.
他坚持住了。

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:18.440
He had a guest of the time.
当时他有一位客人。

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:19.440
That was full.
那已经满了。

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:20.440
That was Marshall.
那是马歇尔。

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:25.440
And it didn't even occur to me until right now we've been talking for a long time.
直到现在我们已经聊了很长时间，我才意识到这一点。

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.319
I know, I'm flattered.
我知道，我受宠若惊。

00:18:27.319 --> 00:18:29.359
I'm flattered, it didn't come up earlier.
我很受宠若惊，它没有早点出现。

00:18:29.359 --> 00:18:41.400
There's a deeper thing at play, which is the writers and the actors got to know each other and were friends and really like symbiotic, it was a symbiotic relationship, you know?
还有更深层次的事情在起作用，那就是编剧和演员互相认识，成为朋友，真的很喜欢共生，这是一种共生关系，你知道吗？

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:42.519
Absolutely, yeah.
绝对是的。

00:18:42.519 --> 00:18:49.359
And then stories went back and forth and things that actors, that stories came out of things that happened on stage.
然后故事来来回回，演员的故事来自舞台上发生的事情。

00:18:49.359 --> 00:18:50.200
It was awesome.
太棒了。

00:18:50.200 --> 00:18:57.880
And just to say, not to sound too back in my day or too like pro-union guy, this is what the writer's guild was striking for, in part, right?
只是想说，不要听起来太回到我那个时代，也不要太像支持工会的人，这就是作家协会罢工的部分目的，对吗？

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:05.019
The idea that writers should be on shoots, writers shouldn't pre-write things and then disappear and then it's shot and they're separate.
编剧应该参与拍摄的想法，编剧不应该预先写好东西，然后消失，然后拍摄，他们就分开了。

00:19:05.019 --> 00:19:10.880
The actors and the writers getting to know each other actually impacts the final part.
演员和编剧的相互了解实际上影响了最后的部分。

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:19.440
And when Chris was on his day for his own episodes, he would come up with all lines that were hilarious and made it into the episodes.
当克里斯在他自己的剧集中时，他会想出所有搞笑的台词并将其纳入剧集中。

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:27.079
I remember I would accuse you, I would say, you would say, back when I was at Stanford, Do you think Ted has to cross on that line, or should he cross on that line?
我记得我会指责你，我会说，你会说，当我在斯坦福大学的时候，你认为特德必须越过那条线，还是他应该越过那条线？

00:19:27.079 --> 00:19:31.759
I was like, Chris, you didn't need to say back when I was in Stanford.
我当时想，克里斯，当我在斯坦福大学的时候，你不需要说回来。

00:19:31.759 --> 00:19:33.839
I really don't think I said that.
我真的不认为我说过这样的话。

00:19:33.839 --> 00:19:37.359
I can't believe we're having this argument again.
我不敢相信我们又发生这样的争吵。

00:19:37.359 --> 00:19:39.359
Yeah.
是的。

00:19:39.359 --> 00:19:43.039
Well, Chris, we're delighted we have the door is open.
好吧，克里斯，我们很高兴门是开着的。

00:19:43.039 --> 00:19:47.200
Please come back and talk about how I made it by the way again.
请回来顺便说说我是怎么做到的。

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:49.960
